      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Associations have been reported between irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "To explore the inter-relations between these conditions in a general population sample."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "A randomly selected community sample of 5492 adults."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "All of the subjects were invited for a medical interview. Those subjects suspected to have either asthma and/or IBS underwent spirometry with post-bronchodilator test if indicated. The labeling of subjects as having IBS was based on Rome II criteria."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "4762 subjects agreed to take part (86.7% response). Prevalence rates for IBS, and currently active asthma were 7.1% and 3.8%, respectively. Logistic regression showed independent associations between IBS and most asthma symptom categories. There was no significant independent association between IBS and chronic bronchitis. The odds ratio with 95% confidence interval (CI) for IBS and current asthma was 1.79 (1.06-3.03)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These observations indicate the necessity of further community-based studies to elucidate the possible common pathogenic mechanisms involved in two disease entities."
        }
      ]},
